# FixedCropper

`FixedCropper` is a cropper whose stencil has a fixed size on screen, set in pixels by `stencilSize`. Instead of resizing the stencil, the user moves and zooms the image underneath it, which is the familiar behaviour of avatar and cover-photo pickers on mobile. It accepts the same props and has the same methods and callbacks as [`Cropper`](/docs/components/Cropper), with the differences below.

<Example {source} title="FixedCropperDemo">
	<FixedCropperDemo />
</Example>

## Usage

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import { FixedCropper, ImageRestriction, type FixedCropperRef } from 'svelte-advanced-cropper';

	let cropper: FixedCropperRef | undefined = $state();
</script>

<FixedCropper
	bind:this={cropper}
	{src}
	stencilSize={{ width: 300, height: 300 }}
	stencilProps={{ handlers: {}, lines: {}, movable: false, resizable: false }}
	imageRestriction={ImageRestriction.stencil}
/>
```

The `stencilProps` hide the handlers and lines and lock the stencil in place, which gives a purely fixed cropper. Leave them out and the user can still move and resize the stencil, but it returns to the given size and to the center once they let go: that is the hybrid cropper described in [Cropper Types](/docs/guides/cropper-types#hybrid-cropper). `ImageRestriction.stencil` lets the user reach every part of the image. With the default `fitArea`, some areas near the edges can't be placed under the stencil.

## Props

Every [`Cropper` prop](/docs/components/Cropper#props) is accepted, plus `stencilSize`. The `aspectRatio` and `sizeRestrictions` props are left out of the types, because the stencil size determines both.

### `stencilSize`

**Type:** `Size | ((state: CropperState, settings) => Size)` · **Required**

```ts
interface Size {
	width: number;
	height: number;
}
```

The size of the stencil in boundary (on-screen) pixels. The aspect ratio of the crop follows from it, combined with the stencil's own aspect ratio if it has one (a `CircleStencil` stays round). If the boundary is smaller than this size, the stencil is scaled down to fit, keeping its proportions. A function receives the current state, so it can derive the size from the boundary, as the demo above does:

```ts
const stencilSize = ({ boundary }: CropperState) => ({
	width: Math.min(300, boundary.width - 40),
	height: Math.min(300, boundary.height - 40)
});
```

### Defaults that differ from `Cropper`

| What                                     | `FixedCropper` default                                              | Can you override it?                     |
| ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| `postProcess`                            | `fixedStencil`: zooms and recenters so the stencil keeps its size        | Yes, but you lose the fixed size         |
| `stencilConstraints`                     | `fixedStencilConstraints`: combines `stencilSize` with the stencil  | Yes                                      |
| `defaultSize`                            | the stencil size, converted to image pixels                         | Yes                                      |
| `aspectRatio`                            | the ratio of `stencilSize`                                          | Only at runtime (not in the types)       |
| `sizeRestrictions`                       | derived from `stencilSize`                                          | Only at runtime (not in the types)       |
| `transformImage.adjustStencil`           | always `false`                                                      | No                                       |

These defaults come from the `stencil-size` extension of the core (`advanced-cropper/extensions/stencil-size`).

## Methods

`bind:this` gives a `FixedCropperRef`, which has every [`Cropper` method](/docs/components/Cropper#methods). `getSettings()` also returns `stencilSize`, already wrapped into a function.

## Callbacks

The same as [`Cropper`'s callbacks](/docs/components/Cropper#callbacks). Each receives the `FixedCropperRef`.

<Admonition type="tip" title="Building a custom fixed cropper">

The [Absolute Zoom](/docs/tutorials/absolute-zoom) tutorial adds a zoom slider to a `FixedCropper` through a custom `wrapperComponent`.

</Admonition>
